<GuideLayout
  title="Sleeping mat R-values without the jargon"
  description="Why insulation under you matters, what the number means and how to choose a mat for the trip."
  eyebrow="Sleep system"
  readTime="5 min read"
>
  <h2>R-value measures resistance to heat flow</h2><p>
    A higher R-value means more resistance to heat moving from your body into
    the ground. It is an insulation measure, not a comfort score. Thickness,
    surface shape and fabric feel still affect how comfortable a mat is to lie
    on.
  </p>
  <h2>Think in broad condition bands</h2><p>
    For mild warm-weather use, a lower R-value may be enough. Cooler
    three-season conditions need more insulation, while winter and snow camping
    demand a much more conservative system. Manufacturer guidance and current
    standardised test data should inform the final choice.
  </p>
  <h2>Mats can be combined</h2><p>
    R-values can usually be added when two mats are layered. A closed-cell foam
    mat under an inflatable can add warmth, protect against punctures and
    provide a basic backup if the inflatable fails.
  </p>
  <h2>Warmth and comfort are separate decisions</h2><p>
    A thick inflatable mat may feel plush yet provide modest insulation, while a
    thinner insulated design may be warmer. Check both the measured R-value and
    dimensions that suit your body and sleeping position.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
